<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Sforza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1401">1401</a>–<a href="/years/1466">1466</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">House of Sforza</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Sforza was an Italian condottiere, son of Muzio Attendolo Sforza, who married Bianca Maria, daughter of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. After the duke's death in 1447 he commanded the army of the Golden Ambrosian Republic, then turned against it and entered Milan as duke in 1450, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza dynasty</a>. He joined the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> of 1454, while his brother Alessandro became lord of Pesaro.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza.jpg" alt="Francesco Sforza" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: School of Lombardy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Sforza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco Sforza was born at Cigoli, near San Miniato in Tuscany, one of the seven illegitimate sons of the condottiero Muzio Sforza and Lucia de Martini. He spent his childhood at Tricarico in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilicata">Basilicata</a>, whose marquisate King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ladislaus-of-naples">Ladislaus of Naples</a> granted him in 1412. In 1418 he married Polissena Ruffo, a Calabrese noblewoman, and from 1419 he fought beside his father, gaining a name for bending metal bars with his bare hands. He later proved an expert tactician and a skilled field commander.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his father's death during the War of L'Aquila, Sforza took part in the final defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braccio-da-montone">Braccio da Montone</a>, then served the Neapolitan army, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-maria-visconti">Filippo Maria Visconti</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Pay governed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a>'s loyalty: in 1433-1435 he led the Milanese attack on the Papal States but, after conquering Ancona, changed sides and obtained the title of vicar of the city from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-eugene-iv">Pope Eugene IV</a>. He served both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in the later 1430s, and for a time was held prisoner at the castle of Mortara after falling into disgrace.</p><p>On 25 October 1441 he married Bianca Maria at Cremona, under the agreements that ended the war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He then allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, pretender to the Neapolitan throne, and marched south, before defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-piccinino">Niccolò Piccinino</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a> and the Venetians. At the Battle of Montolmo in 1444 he defeated Francesco Piccinino, and was rewarded with the title of capitano generale of the duchy's armies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Famine and riots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> led its senate to entrust him with the duchy, and he entered the city as duke on 26 February 1450, the first time a lay institution had handed over such a title. He never obtained investiture from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>; that came only in 1494, when Emperor Maximilian I invested his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-sforza">Ludovico Sforza</a>. As duke he built an efficient system of taxation, founded the Ospedale Maggiore in Milan, restored the Palazzo ducale and had the Naviglio d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a> dug. His friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de' Medici</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> led to the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-league">Italian League</a>, a defensive alliance that stabilised most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iv-of-england">Edward IV of England</a> made him a knight of the Garter in 1463. Sforza suffered from hydropsy and gout, and when a rumour of his death in 1462 set off a riot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, he survived four more years, dying in March 1466. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galeazzo-maria-sforza">Galeazzo Maria Sforza</a> succeeded him. Ludovico later commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> to design an equestrian monument to him; Leonardo finished a clay horse in 1492, but the statue was never built.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco I Sforza” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious life centred on the Ambrosian rite, unique to the archdiocese, and on great building projects: Gian Galeazzo began Milan Cathedral in 1386 and the Certosa di Pavia in 1396, and <b>Francesco Sforza</b> founded the Ospedale Maggiore, designed by Filarete, in 1456.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Threatened by the Republic of Venice, it hired <b>Francesco Sforza</b>, husband of the late duke's illegitimate daughter Bianca Maria, who defeated the Venetians at Caravaggio in 1448.</blockquote><a href="/state/golden-ambrosian-republic">Golden Ambrosian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1445 Galeazzo Malatesta sold Pesaro to Alessandro Sforza, brother of <b>Francesco Sforza</b>, who had married his granddaughter Costanza da Varano.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pesaro">Lordship of Pesaro</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Miniato, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.68,10.85&amp;year=1443" aria-label="San Miniato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 615 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francesco Sforza"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5261 885h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5261 885h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.68,10.85&amp;year=1443">What was here in 1443? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
